WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.816 --> 00:00:03.603
(air swoosh)

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:04.773
- What's up, Believe Nation?

00:00:04.773 --> 00:00:06.641
It's Evan, my one word is believe

00:00:06.641 --> 00:00:08.545
and I believe in people
more than they believe

00:00:08.545 --> 00:00:09.777
in themselves.

00:00:09.777 --> 00:00:12.454
And my sincere hope is
that if you see in you

00:00:12.454 --> 00:00:16.240
what I see in you, you'll be
able to change the planet.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:17.966
I started the Meant to
Me series with the goal

00:00:17.966 --> 00:00:20.211
to try to learn from people
who've done a lot more than us.

00:00:20.211 --> 00:00:22.832
Try to hang out with people
who've had more success than us

00:00:22.832 --> 00:00:24.962
and hopefully some of their beliefs,

00:00:24.962 --> 00:00:26.954
some of their mindsets,
some of their way of working

00:00:26.954 --> 00:00:30.353
seeps into us to help us
become the best version

00:00:30.353 --> 00:00:31.767
of ourselves.

00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:33.496
So today we're going to
learn from Malcolm Glagwell

00:00:33.496 --> 00:00:35.563
and his outliers advice.

00:00:35.563 --> 00:00:36.396
MentorMe, Malcolm.

00:00:36.396 --> 00:00:38.134
Rule number two is my personal favorite

00:00:38.134 --> 00:00:40.734
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:42.621
Also as you're watching,
if you hear something

00:00:42.621 --> 00:00:44.028
that really resonates with you,

00:00:44.028 --> 00:00:45.677
please leave it down the comments below

00:00:45.677 --> 00:00:47.278
and put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired

00:00:47.278 --> 00:00:48.644
by it as well.

00:00:48.644 --> 00:00:50.309
And if you leave it within
the first couple hours

00:00:50.309 --> 00:00:52.317
of this video going live,
you have a chance to win

00:00:52.317 --> 00:00:54.317
one of two daily prizes.

00:00:55.502 --> 00:00:58.419
(orchestral music)

00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:10.530
- An outlier, if you do some
sort of scientific experiment,

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.478
there's this thing out there,

00:01:12.478 --> 00:01:15.470
that is sort of this aberration
that is way off the charts

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:16.924
and that's an outlier, right?

00:01:16.924 --> 00:01:17.757
- Yes, that's right.

00:01:17.757 --> 00:01:19.916
- OK, so let's talk about some outliers.

00:01:19.916 --> 00:01:24.166
One of the examples of
success you use in the book

00:01:24.166 --> 00:01:25.369
are The Beatles.

00:01:25.369 --> 00:01:26.202
- Yeah.

00:01:26.202 --> 00:01:29.830
- Tell the story of The
Beatles and why they defy

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:33.065
the norms and why they were
as successful as they were.

00:01:33.065 --> 00:01:36.112
- Well I was really taken
by what happened to them

00:01:36.112 --> 00:01:37.605
before they came to America.

00:01:37.605 --> 00:01:41.946
They come here in '64,
instant Beatles invasion.

00:01:41.946 --> 00:01:42.779
- [Harry] Explode.

00:01:42.779 --> 00:01:43.711
- [Malcome] Explode.

00:01:43.711 --> 00:01:46.106
But before they came,
they were the house band

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:48.133
in a strip club in Homburg, Germany.

00:01:48.133 --> 00:01:48.966
- [Harry] Right.

00:01:48.966 --> 00:01:52.378
- [Malcom] And they would
play eight hour sets,

00:01:52.378 --> 00:01:54.000
seven days a week for months at a stretch.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.569
And the idea there is that is what that,

00:01:55.569 --> 00:01:57.942
we think of them as these
geniuses who just come out

00:01:57.942 --> 00:02:00.266
of nowhere and who are
naturally brilliant,

00:02:00.266 --> 00:02:03.484
but in fact they put in an
apprenticeship of thousands

00:02:03.484 --> 00:02:04.801
of hours before--

00:02:04.801 --> 00:02:06.391
- These overnight successes

00:02:06.391 --> 00:02:08.743
aren't overnight successes at all.

00:02:08.743 --> 00:02:10.413
- Yeah, that's one of the big
themes of the book, actually,

00:02:10.413 --> 00:02:12.968
is that we sometimes look
at people and we think

00:02:12.968 --> 00:02:14.643
they must have this great innate talent

00:02:14.643 --> 00:02:16.561
and we forget the enormous
amount of work that went

00:02:16.561 --> 00:02:19.706
in to what looks like genius.

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:21.021
There's a principle I
talk about in the book

00:02:21.021 --> 00:02:23.182
called the Ten Thousand
Hour Rule, which says that

00:02:23.182 --> 00:02:26.484
in a wide variety of disciplines,
from being a good doctor

00:02:26.484 --> 00:02:31.141
to being a great golfer, it
doesn't matter really what

00:02:31.141 --> 00:02:34.669
it is, it looks like we
need to spend 10,000 hours

00:02:34.669 --> 00:02:37.442
practicing before we get good.

00:02:37.442 --> 00:02:39.831
So there's never been a
great classical music,

00:02:39.831 --> 00:02:41.654
piece of classical music
composed by someone who

00:02:41.654 --> 00:02:43.412
hasn't spent 10,000 hours first.

00:02:43.412 --> 00:02:45.219
- Well, you write about
Mozart and everybody talks

00:02:45.219 --> 00:02:47.209
about Mozart composing as a little kid.

00:02:47.209 --> 00:02:49.962
He didn't really compose
anything anybody cared about

00:02:49.962 --> 00:02:51.276
until he was what?

00:02:51.276 --> 00:02:53.382
- 23, at which point he had been composing

00:02:53.382 --> 00:02:54.370
for over 10 years.

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.027
(Harry laughs)

00:02:56.027 --> 00:02:58.115
- So it really, really does make sense.

00:02:58.115 --> 00:03:01.866
- I remain, I'm
uninterested in that topic.

00:03:01.866 --> 00:03:03.197
- [Charlie] Which one?

00:03:03.197 --> 00:03:05.412
The relation between gift and practice?

00:03:05.412 --> 00:03:08.237
- No I'm more interested in natural gifts.

00:03:08.237 --> 00:03:09.827
I know they exist and I
know there's such a thing

00:03:09.827 --> 00:03:12.024
as talent, but blah blah blah,

00:03:12.024 --> 00:03:14.630
but I just feel like, and so what?

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:17.819
Because there people
and I always think of,

00:03:17.819 --> 00:03:20.095
you're a basketball fan, I
always think of Derrick Coleman.

00:03:20.095 --> 00:03:20.951
- Yes.

00:03:20.951 --> 00:03:23.173
- I remember chatting with a
guy who worked for the '6ers

00:03:23.173 --> 00:03:24.006
and he said,

00:03:24.006 --> 00:03:25.822
"You have to understand
that Derrick Coleman

00:03:25.822 --> 00:03:29.187
was the most gifted man ever to set foot

00:03:29.187 --> 00:03:30.502
on a basketball court."

00:03:30.502 --> 00:03:31.510
He said,

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:35.154
"You've heard million other
names, nobody was as good

00:03:35.154 --> 00:03:35.987
as Derrick."

00:03:35.987 --> 00:03:38.886
Who has heard today, who
thinks, who puts Derrick Coleman

00:03:38.886 --> 00:03:39.866
up there in the pantheon?

00:03:39.866 --> 00:03:40.699
No one.

00:03:40.699 --> 00:03:42.209
He didn't want to work.

00:03:42.209 --> 00:03:44.629
He had bad habits, he had a bad attitude.

00:03:44.629 --> 00:03:45.807
I mean, so what?

00:03:45.807 --> 00:03:48.930
Give me somebody who wants to
go to practice in the morning

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.260
and who wants to try in games
and I'll want to celebrate him.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:55.534
When you put forth effort, you get reward.

00:03:55.534 --> 00:03:57.918
When you throw your
heart and mind and soul

00:03:57.918 --> 00:04:01.400
into something, you get something back.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.983
Now in my book, I call
that notion, that belief,

00:04:03.983 --> 00:04:08.202
that effort brings reward Meaningful Work.

00:04:08.202 --> 00:04:10.062
And when you look at the lives of people

00:04:10.062 --> 00:04:12.887
who are really successful, what
you see over and over again

00:04:12.887 --> 00:04:16.306
is this idea of meaningful
work is embedded

00:04:16.306 --> 00:04:18.223
in their consciousness.

00:04:19.286 --> 00:04:24.233
You know, I tell the story
in the book of The Beatles.

00:04:24.233 --> 00:04:26.393
Everybody knows about The
Beatles, they come here in 1964,

00:04:26.393 --> 00:04:27.899
and the British Invasion.

00:04:27.899 --> 00:04:29.724
The most interesting
thing about the Beatles

00:04:29.724 --> 00:04:33.505
is what happened to them
before they came to America.

00:04:33.505 --> 00:04:37.092
In 1960, 1959, when they were just kids,

00:04:37.092 --> 00:04:40.799
they were invited to go
to Homburg in Germany

00:04:40.799 --> 00:04:44.311
to be the house band at a strip club.

00:04:44.311 --> 00:04:45.882
And they went there and
they stayed there for months

00:04:45.882 --> 00:04:50.049
on end and seven days a week,
they played eight hour sets,

00:04:52.739 --> 00:04:56.161
night after night in
this strip club, right?

00:04:56.161 --> 00:04:59.409
And over the course of that
extraordinary crucible,

00:04:59.409 --> 00:05:02.459
that experience of playing,
they taught themselves

00:05:02.459 --> 00:05:04.862
how to be a great band.

00:05:04.862 --> 00:05:07.603
In fact, we know, we think now,
that by the time The Beatles

00:05:07.603 --> 00:05:10.016
came to America, they had
played together as a live band

00:05:10.016 --> 00:05:11.016
1,200 times.

00:05:12.687 --> 00:05:14.957
We could go to all of
the clubs on Friday night

00:05:14.957 --> 00:05:17.704
in San Francisco, where all
of the clubs where all the

00:05:17.704 --> 00:05:18.916
promising young bands are playing,

00:05:18.916 --> 00:05:20.943
I submit to you, you would
not find a single band

00:05:20.943 --> 00:05:23.579
that has played together
1,200 times, right?

00:05:23.579 --> 00:05:25.658
Just doesn't happen.

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:28.408
So what made The Beatles special?

00:05:28.408 --> 00:05:31.988
What made them special
is that they were willing

00:05:31.988 --> 00:05:34.047
to play together 1,200 times.

00:05:34.047 --> 00:05:37.084
Willing to play eight hour
sets, seven nights a week

00:05:37.084 --> 00:05:38.628
for months at a stretch.

00:05:38.628 --> 00:05:40.419
And why were they willing,
because they believed

00:05:40.419 --> 00:05:43.139
in the notion of meaningful work.

00:05:43.139 --> 00:05:45.697
They had an opportunity to
throw their heart and mind

00:05:45.697 --> 00:05:48.864
into something and get something back.

00:05:49.892 --> 00:05:52.169
And that made all the
difference in the world.

00:05:52.169 --> 00:05:55.974
And to my annoyance,
some reviewers of my book

00:05:55.974 --> 00:05:59.767
have accused me of being
a cultural determinist.

00:05:59.767 --> 00:06:01.934
In fact, I'm the opposite.

00:06:02.866 --> 00:06:05.457
The culture that we come
from is only deterministic

00:06:05.457 --> 00:06:09.540
of our behavior if we
choose to ignore it, right?

00:06:10.476 --> 00:06:14.093
If you never address, they
chapter that I was talking about

00:06:14.093 --> 00:06:16.757
in my talk, the plane crash chapter,

00:06:16.757 --> 00:06:20.014
it's really about Korean
Air and how Korean Air goes

00:06:20.014 --> 00:06:23.023
from being an airline that
almost gets pushed out

00:06:23.023 --> 00:06:25.511
of business because it
has so many plane crashes,

00:06:25.511 --> 00:06:28.425
to being one of the, now
today, one of the premier

00:06:28.425 --> 00:06:29.983
airlines in the world.

00:06:29.983 --> 00:06:33.220
And they transformed themselves
over the last 10 years

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:37.733
precisely because they say
they decide, at long last,

00:06:37.733 --> 00:06:41.796
to confront their cultural
legacy and deal with it.

00:06:41.796 --> 00:06:44.000
And what they discover is
if they are honest and open

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.760
about the fact that in
this particular instance,

00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:49.927
the cockpit, acting like you're
a Korean is not a good idea.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:52.457
Then you can change it, and they do.

00:06:52.457 --> 00:06:55.769
They take that airline from,
literally, it was this close

00:06:55.769 --> 00:07:00.222
to not existing anymore
and is now an absolutely

00:07:00.222 --> 00:07:01.384
world class airline.

00:07:01.384 --> 00:07:03.798
And what they've showed,
and I continue this theme

00:07:03.798 --> 00:07:05.623
in the second half of the book is,

00:07:05.623 --> 00:07:08.632
once we can talk about
and confront culture,

00:07:08.632 --> 00:07:09.743
we can change culture.

00:07:09.743 --> 00:07:11.017
We're not prisoners of it.

00:07:11.017 --> 00:07:15.129
We're only prisoners of it if
we pretend it doesn't exist.

00:07:15.129 --> 00:07:17.725
I had a conversation a couple weeks ago,

00:07:17.725 --> 00:07:21.307
I was giving a talk and I
was seated next to a guy

00:07:21.307 --> 00:07:24.687
who ran a regional bank in Akron, Ohio

00:07:24.687 --> 00:07:27.083
and I said to him, talking
about his business,

00:07:27.083 --> 00:07:28.565
"How's your business?
Your banking business?"

00:07:28.565 --> 00:07:29.398
He says,

00:07:29.398 --> 00:07:30.231
"Oh, we're fine.

00:07:30.231 --> 00:07:31.508
In fact, we're more than
fine, we're about to buy

00:07:31.508 --> 00:07:32.868
a big bank in Chicago."

00:07:32.868 --> 00:07:33.766
And I said,

00:07:33.766 --> 00:07:35.004
"Well why are you fine
and no one else is?"

00:07:35.004 --> 00:07:37.912
He was an older man, he was
probably in his, I'd say 60s.

00:07:37.912 --> 00:07:39.122
And he said,

00:07:39.122 --> 00:07:41.783
"I've been through this
three times before."

00:07:41.783 --> 00:07:43.442
And I suspect, we sort of talked a bit,

00:07:43.442 --> 00:07:47.863
and suspect that he got
humbled 25 years ago,

00:07:47.863 --> 00:07:50.308
or in the early 70s or late 70s,

00:07:50.308 --> 00:07:52.605
and never forgot that lesson.

00:07:52.605 --> 00:07:56.984
And it's that kind of,
it is in times like that

00:07:56.984 --> 00:07:59.734
that we understand why experience

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:03.706
and learning from
experience is so important.

00:08:03.706 --> 00:08:05.798
It's more than simply, that word is not

00:08:05.798 --> 00:08:08.548
a kind of meaningless triviality.

00:08:09.618 --> 00:08:11.993
Experience matters because
there's certain kinds

00:08:11.993 --> 00:08:14.509
of things that you only learned
when you've been humbled,

00:08:14.509 --> 00:08:15.359
right?

00:08:15.359 --> 00:08:18.788
You can't just explain to a
28 year-old things are going

00:08:18.788 --> 00:08:22.016
to get bad, it's not going to sink in.

00:08:22.016 --> 00:08:26.574
But to this man I was speaking
to, who saw it first hand

00:08:26.574 --> 00:08:29.247
and dealt with it and I'm
sure went through all manner

00:08:29.247 --> 00:08:31.112
of crisis before,

00:08:31.112 --> 00:08:34.262
it's a lesson that he
kept with him, you know?

00:08:34.262 --> 00:08:37.845
Colin Powell, before
the Iraq war, was the,

00:08:39.898 --> 00:08:41.552
he was the in-house skeptic.

00:08:41.552 --> 00:08:42.385
Why?

00:08:42.385 --> 00:08:43.921
Because he'd been through Vietnam.

00:08:43.921 --> 00:08:46.593
You know, in a very first-hand
way and a way that many

00:08:46.593 --> 00:08:50.092
of the other decision
makers had not and had never

00:08:50.092 --> 00:08:52.067
forgotten those lessons.

00:08:52.067 --> 00:08:56.234
There's another sort of case
of someone who appropriately

00:08:57.634 --> 00:09:00.936
was humbled and learned from experience.

00:09:00.936 --> 00:09:03.593
And you've got to have
people like that around.

00:09:03.593 --> 00:09:06.984
All my work is about storytelling,

00:09:06.984 --> 00:09:10.738
but using stories to kind
of get at deeper truths.

00:09:10.738 --> 00:09:13.408
But fundamentally I'm a
storyteller and every,

00:09:13.408 --> 00:09:15.106
you know in revisionist history,

00:09:15.106 --> 00:09:18.106
everyone of the episodes is a story.

00:09:21.409 --> 00:09:23.593
It's not me ranting on about this or that

00:09:23.593 --> 00:09:25.062
and the other thing (laughs).

00:09:25.062 --> 00:09:27.723
It's we meet really interesting people

00:09:27.723 --> 00:09:29.975
and we tell their stories
and we use that as a way of,

00:09:29.975 --> 00:09:32.178
and that's a theme in all of my work.

00:09:32.178 --> 00:09:36.498
I think that the way
into complicated ideas is

00:09:36.498 --> 00:09:39.248
through people and their stories.

00:09:40.665 --> 00:09:42.001
